MIM DB Workbench — Professional Visual-First MongoDB Client

Enhance the EXISTING MIM DB Workbench into a powerful, intuitive, Studio 3T-inspired MongoDB querying experience, while retaining its MIM-specific AI capabilities.

Primary goal: Make everyday MongoDB querying extremely easy through mouse-driven interactions, without requiring users to remember field names or MongoDB syntax.

This is NOT a request to clone Studio 3T’s proprietary UI or code. Build an original, professional experience offering equivalent useful workflows.

1. First Inspect Existing Architecture

Before implementation, inspect:

* Existing Angular DB Workbench pages/components.
* Node backend MongoDB integration.
* Existing authentication/OIDC and read-only policies.
* Natural-language query generation and execution.
* Table/Tree/JSON viewers.
* Existing query validation, pagination and persistence.
* Installed npm packages and existing UI components.

Reuse and enhance existing functionality. Avoid duplicate components, redundant APIs, paid dependencies and unnecessary architectural changes.

Do not break existing MongoDB connectivity, MIM Expert chat integration, authentication or other application modules.

2. UX Philosophy — Visual First, Minimal Interface

The default DB Workbench must be extremely clean.

Prioritize the most frequent tasks:

1. Select a database and collection.
2. Browse existing documents.
3. Filter by an exact field value.
4. Apply one-click sorting.
5. Add or remove filter conditions.
6. Switch between Table, Tree and JSON views.
7. Execute and inspect results.

Common tasks should require only one or two clicks.

Advanced features must be available through contextual menus, expandable panels and clearly organized modes, without overwhelming ordinary users.

Use resizable panels, sensible defaults, keyboard shortcuts, loading states, empty states and a responsive layout.

3. Studio 3T-Style Interactive Results Grid

Implement a professional MongoDB results grid with:

* Resizable and reorderable columns.
* One-click ascending/descending sorting.
* Click cycle: Ascending → Descending → No Sort.
* Shift+click for multi-column sorting.
* Server-side filtering, sorting and pagination.
* Column visibility and projection controls.
* Expandable nested BSON objects and arrays.
* Correct display of ObjectId, Date, Decimal128, Boolean, Null and arrays.
* Copy cell value, field path, document and JSON.
* Table, Tree and JSON display modes.
* Adjustable result limits and efficient rendering.
* Clear loading, timeout, error and empty-result feedback.

Sorting must operate on the MongoDB query, not merely on currently visible rows.

Prefer existing grid components. Evaluate AG Grid Community only if it offers clear benefits and is approved/available internally.

Do not introduce AG Grid Enterprise or other paid dependencies.

4. Drag-and-Drop Query Building

This is a CRITICAL feature.

Users must be able to drag existing field names or document values from Table/Tree views directly into a Filter Builder.

Examples:

Drag status = FAILED into Filter:

Automatically create:

{ "status": "FAILED" }

Drag processing.retryCount = 3:

Automatically recognize the complete nested field path and numeric BSON type.

The user may then change the operator to:

Equals, Not Equals, Greater Than, Less Than, Greater/Equal, Less/Equal, Between, In, Not In, Exists, Contains, Starts With or other compatible operations.

Only display operators appropriate to the selected field’s datatype.

Support dragging fields into:

* Filter conditions.
* Sort criteria.
* Projection/column selection.

Provide visual drop targets, hover highlighting, drag previews and removable/editable filter chips.

Use Angular CDK drag-drop if appropriate.

No AI call is allowed for drag-and-drop, filtering, sorting or ordinary query execution.

5. Powerful Right-Click Context Menus

Right-clicking a cell or field must expose useful context-aware actions.

For example:

* Filter Equals This Value.
* Filter Not Equals This Value.
* Greater Than / Less Than when applicable.
* Filter Null / Not Null.
* Filter by Field Existence.
* Add to Existing Filter.
* Sort Ascending / Descending.
* Copy Value.
* Copy Field Name / Full Field Path.
* Copy Document as JSON.
* Show/Hide Column.
* Add Field to Projection.
* Inspect Full Value.

Nested fields must retain correct paths.

Example: processing.retryCount, not merely retryCount.

Right-click actions should be fast, intuitive, and should never generate Kiro requests.

Use proper menu positioning and keyboard accessibility.

6. Smart Visual Filter Builder

Create a compact, intuitive query builder.

Support:

* Field selection with autocomplete.
* Type-aware value input.
* AND/OR conditions and nested condition groups.
* Add, edit, remove and reorder conditions.
* Filter chips with clear labels.
* Clear individual/all filters.
* Quick exact-value filtering.
* Visual sorting rules.
* Projection controls.
* Generated MongoDB query preview.

Allow users to edit filters without manually modifying JSON.

Support valid BSON values, nested fields and arrays.

A newly added filter should not automatically execute unexpectedly; provide explicit Apply/Run behavior, with a clear indication of pending changes.

Keep the interface compact by default.

7. Three Connected Query Modes

Retain and integrate the following modes:

A. Natural Language

Keep existing human-language querying.

Example:

“Find failed participants having more than two retries.”

AI generates a safe structured read-only query.

B. Visual Builder — Default

Show generated conditions visually.

Users can further refine queries through mouse-driven actions.

C. Advanced Query Editor

Provide a professional MongoDB query editor with syntax highlighting, autocomplete, field suggestions, formatting, validation and useful errors.

Support read-only find queries and permitted aggregation pipelines.

Reuse the existing Monaco/editor integration where possible.

All three modes should share a canonical structured query representation where supported.

Natural Language → Visual Builder → Advanced Editor should work without losing compatible query conditions.

When a complex advanced query cannot be accurately represented in Visual Builder, preserve it and display a clear non-destructive compatibility message. Never silently remove or rewrite unsupported operators.

8. Advanced Querying & Aggregation

Allow advanced users to execute supported MongoDB read-only queries.

Include:

* Find with filters, sort, limit and projection.
* Type-aware expressions.
* Nested document and array filtering.
* Advanced structured conditions.
* Read-only aggregation pipelines.
* Visual aggregation stage editing.
* Enable/disable/reorder pipeline stages.
* Explicit bounded preview of intermediate stages.
* Query validation and meaningful errors.
* Query execution timing.
* Index information and permitted Explain Plan inspection.

Do not make these capabilities dominate the default UI.

Provide advanced functionality progressively when the user requests it.

9. Smart Query Memory — NO Visible Query History

This is another CRITICAL UX requirement.

Executed queries should be remembered automatically in persistent local storage, but there must be NO permanent History panel, Saved Queries sidebar, favorites list or historical-query table cluttering the interface.

Instead, implement Contextual Query Suggestions.

Example:

A user previously executed:

db.participants.find({status:"FAILED",retryCount:{$gt:2}})

Later, when typing a related expression, relevant past queries appear as a small autocomplete suggestion dropdown.

Requirements:

* Save successful user-executed read-only query definitions locally.
* Deduplicate equivalent queries using normalized query fingerprints.
* Match by partial expression, field name, operators and relevant text.
* Support suggestions for Natural Language and Advanced Editor inputs.
* Prefer same connection, environment, database and collection.
* Never accidentally suggest a PROD query as an equivalent DEV query without clearly distinguishing its context.
* Show a maximum of 3–5 highly relevant suggestions.
* Suggestions only appear during meaningful typing or explicit autocomplete invocation.
* Hide suggestions when irrelevant, dismissed or unfocused.
* Selecting a suggestion fills the editor; it must NOT execute immediately.
* Do not automatically run history queries.
* Do not display old queries on page load.
* Provide a discreet way to remove a suggestion.
* Provide history retention/clear settings under Configuration or Settings, not in the primary workspace.
* Avoid saving sensitive document values or secrets where inappropriate; support redaction and configurable retention limits.

Use local deterministic indexing/fuzzy matching, such as MiniSearch if beneficial.

No Kiro calls for query-history suggestions.

Also provide field-name and operator autocomplete based on available collection metadata.

10. Smart Quick Search

Provide an unobtrusive quick-filter experience for common use cases:

* Search by known field such as participantId.
* Search by ObjectId.
* Search by an exact value.
* Reuse the active collection’s known fields.
* Provide field suggestions while typing.
* Support quick conversion into a persistent visual filter.

Do not assume an input value matches every database field.

Use explicit field selection when necessary, with schema-aware suggestions.

A simple query should never require opening the Advanced Editor.

11. Read-Only Security — Mandatory

Preserve the existing read-only database access model.

Advanced Query Editor must NOT become an unrestricted Mongo shell.

Requirements:

* Never execute arbitrary JavaScript or eval.
* No direct mongosh command execution.
* No insert, update, delete, drop or database modifications.
* Reject $out, $merge, $where, $function, $accumulator, mapReduce and any other potentially unsafe or unsupported operations.
* Enforce strict server-side query validation using allowlisted operations/stages.
* Do not rely solely on frontend validation.
* Execute only approved MongoDB driver operations.
* Preserve existing authentication and database permissions.
* Use least-privilege read-only credentials wherever possible.
* Set query timeouts, maximum result limits and resource limits.
* Support cancellation where practical.
* Prevent unrestricted collection scans or expensive queries where possible.
* Validate database/collection identifiers and query structure.
* Never expose credentials or connection secrets.
* Avoid exposing data in logs or query-history records.
* Switching environments must not silently rerun a query against another database.

Support explain/index inspection only where authorized.

Keep the implementation compatible with corporate database security policies.

12. MIM-Specific Intelligence

Preserve the existing Ask MIM Expert integration.

Allow users to send selected query results or relevant document context to the main AI agent through an explicit action.

The agent may use existing authorized KB, Java code, API and service context to explain records or investigate issues.

Avoid sending entire large collections to Kiro.

Only transmit selected, minimized and permission-appropriate data.

Ordinary querying must remain deterministic and use zero Kiro credits.

13. Performance & Responsiveness

The Workbench must remain smooth on corporate Windows VDIs.

Implement:

* Lazy database/collection metadata discovery.
* Bounded schema sampling.
* Debounced field suggestions.
* Efficient server-side pagination.
* Stable cursor handling and predictable sorting.
* Virtualized grids where useful.
* Cancellation of stale requests.
* Minimal frontend memory consumption.
* Avoid loading entire collections into browser memory.
* Avoid unnecessary backend calls.
* Reuse existing MongoDB connections safely.

Do not add heavyweight dependencies where existing components already provide the necessary functionality.

14. Integration & Implementation Quality

Reuse existing Angular styling, navigation, theme, Node APIs, session storage and configuration patterns.

Do not create a separate MongoDB client application.

Enhance the current MIM DB Workbench in place.

Keep interfaces consistent with MIM Expert’s professional Windows desktop-style experience.

Preserve existing connection management and Natural Language querying.

Do not change other working modules.

Inspect dependencies and ensure any new npm packages are compatible with the available Artifactory, licensing requirements, bundled Node runtime and existing IExpress release build.

15. End-to-End Acceptance Tests

Validate these actual user journeys:

1. Open a MongoDB collection and browse its documents.
2. Right-click status = FAILED and filter without typing a query.
3. Drag an existing cell into the Filter Builder.
4. Drag a nested numeric field and change its operator.
5. Sort results ascending/descending with one click.
6. Add multiple AND/OR filter conditions.
7. Switch Table, Tree and JSON modes without losing query context.
8. Generate a query through Natural Language and refine it visually.
9. Modify a query through the Advanced Editor.
10. Run a permitted read-only aggregation pipeline.
11. Execute a query, close/reopen the app, and get a contextual suggestion when typing a related query.
12. Confirm that no query-history list or permanent history UI appears.
13. Confirm that selecting a suggestion does not automatically execute it.
14. Verify correct ObjectId, Date, Number, Boolean and nested-field handling.
15. Verify sorting/filtering work across the full server-side result set, not only the currently displayed page.
16. Verify that dangerous/write operations are blocked on the backend.
17. Verify that normal visual interactions consume zero Kiro credits.
18. Verify existing DB connections, Natural Language queries and Ask MIM Expert remain functional.

FINAL TARGET

Transform the existing MIM DB Workbench into a Studio 3T-class, visual-first MongoDB querying workspace optimized for everyday MIM development, QA and production troubleshooting.

Users should be able to open data, right-click values, drag fields into filters, sort with one click, refine queries visually and retrieve past queries through intelligent typing suggestions—all with minimal effort and a clean, uncluttered UI.

Implement real end-to-end functionality, not mock UI controls or placeholder actions.

Prioritize usability, reliability, data safety, performance and zero-credit deterministic operations.





