Upgrade the existing MIM Expert Global Command Palette into a **Smart Universal Search & Command Center**, with ZERO Kiro/AI credit usage for normal searches and commands.

First inspect the existing Angular/Node Command Palette, global repo selector, navigation, backend actions, KB, Web IDE, DB Workbench, Service 360 and API Explorer. Reuse existing architecture; do not create duplicate search components or integrations.

### 1. Unified Search
Enhance the existing top search bar and Ctrl+K palette to search across:
- Pages and available actions
- MIM services/repositories
- KB documents
- Local code files/classes/methods
- Indexed APIs/endpoints
- DB collection metadata
- Saved findings, workflows and recent chats

Show categorized, ranked results with relevant icons, descriptions and direct navigation/actions.

Preserve existing Favorites, keyboard navigation, shortcuts and styling.

### 2. Zero-AI Intent Recognition
Implement a lightweight deterministic intent resolver using keywords, aliases, patterns, fuzzy matching and existing project metadata.

Examples:
- "open participant service" → Service 360
- "build reporting" → Build action for reporting repo
- "show APIs of rebalancer" → API Explorer
- "latest 10 records for PIN 123" → DB Workbench with prefilled verified query context
- "find validateParticipant" → Code search/Web IDE
- "check service health" → OpenShift runtime view

Do not invoke Kiro, LLMs, embedding APIs or external paid AI for search, suggestions, intent routing or deterministic actions.

Never invent a service, API, collection or command when no verified match exists.

### 3. Context Awareness
Use the globally selected repo/service, current page and available context to improve matching.

Explicitly named services override the selected repo.

Preserve the recognized intent, parameters and selected entity when navigating to another page so the user never has to re-enter the same query.

### 4. Local Unified Search Index
Build/reuse a lightweight local searchable index covering available repositories, KB, code symbols, API metadata and application entities.

- No AI-generated embeddings.
- Support partial/fuzzy matches and aliases.
- Incrementally update after relevant repo clone/pull, KB updates and metadata changes.
- Use background indexing without blocking the UI.
- Show indexing/loading states and handle unavailable sources gracefully.
- Avoid repeatedly scanning all repositories for every keystroke.

### 5. Actions and Navigation
Results must perform real actions using existing app mechanisms.

- Navigation/search/view actions execute directly.
- Build/Test/Git modifications/API execution require explicit user action and existing validation/confirmation.
- Never automatically execute destructive/state-changing commands from typed search text.
- Maintain repo isolation and existing security policies.

### 6. AI Fallback — Explicit Only
If no confident deterministic match exists, show relevant suggestions and an optional **“Ask MIM Expert”** action.

Only clicking this action may invoke Kiro for complex reasoning.

Normal search must never silently trigger AI or consume Kiro credits.

Clearly distinguish:
- Local Search — No AI
- Ask MIM Expert — Uses AI

### 7. UX & Performance
- Instant autocomplete and suggestions.
- Debounced search where appropriate.
- Keyboard navigation: Ctrl+K, arrows, Enter, Esc.
- Highlight matching text.
- Recent searches and frequently used actions.
- Empty, loading, indexing and error states.
- Responsive UI consistent with existing application.
- Keep local operations fast and avoid unnecessary API calls.

### 8. Validation
Verify end-to-end with actual MIM repositories and existing app data.

Test:
- navigation and service search
- local code/KB/API search
- natural-language command patterns
- selected-repo context
- correct action execution
- index refresh after repository changes
- no-match AI fallback
- zero Kiro calls during normal search.

Preserve all existing functionality.

**TARGET:** One intelligent global search bar for discovering, navigating and operating the MIM ecosystem, with deterministic execution, minimal latency and zero AI-credit consumption unless the user explicitly chooses MIM Expert.



















